l of that bad debt and remove it  essentially  away from the rest of the financial  system and that allows  china's other financial institutions and  its other banks to continue  with different kinds of banking services  that essentially help china's economy to  continue growing  [Music]  lai xiaomin ran china hua rong from  2012 until 2018. he was  also sometimes known as the god of  wealth  had a background in some of china's top  financial institutions  he had posts in the people's bank of  china as well as china's banking  and insurance regulatory commission  under ly xiaomi's leadership at china  hua rong  he expanded from the firm's original  mandate dealing with bad debts  into things like securities trading and  trusts he also turned to  the uh public debt markets to try and  fund  some of that expansion so under lysol  min  we also saw a massive expansion of the  firm's uh  bond outstanding bonds and those types  of debts were sold to both  mainland onshore investors as well as  global investors  overseas in the form of dollar bonds as  well as u.n  and hong kong dollar denominated bonds  laisamen was sometimes criticized  for just caring about short-term profits  and trying to go way beyond the brief of  what traditionally  a bad bank like  [Music]  he was actually charged with bribery and  taking illicit payments  in the course of the trial it came to  light that  had over the years collected a spate of  luxury cars  of luxurious apartments and expensive  watches  he had an apartment that he had renamed  the supermarket that was later found  to have cabinets that were full of cash  and a lot of this came to light during  the trial  some of which was televised and  became quite a public spectacle  he was executed earlier this year  the former board chairman of one of  china's biggest asset management  companies like xiaomi was executed on  friday  authorities said it was found that he  had  so wang zhang feng was um appointed  afterwards to take over the running of  china hua rong  and essentially to try and get it back  onto a  safe and steady footing that included  things like shaping up its financial  health and pulling back from some of the  riskier areas that lyceum in has  expanded into  under his leadership he was seen to be  certainly not as flamboyant as his  predecessor  but also brought in specifically to try  and introduce a  more serious and professional management  style  at china huarong 2.  concerns first flared at the company at  the  end of march when the firm failed to  release its 2020 annual reports  fundamentally what it means is that  investors and we  don't have a very clear insight into  precisely  how that cleanup has actually gone over  the last year  so investors suddenly started to worry  that there could be some issues with its  financial health  now that was then exacerbated by several  media reports  that there could be a potential  financial restructuring at china huawei  well obviously this has been a saga  now uh last week you know speculation  about a possible debt restructuring sent  its overseas bonds  down to record lows we've got another  ratings downgrade for juarong you've got  to wonder whether  this is a little bit of a delayed  reaction well as we've seen it has been  a rocky april for huarong  bond investors and we have seen those  notes continue to hit fresh lows and in  the wake  of this new york times report china  huarong's  bond prices have been incredibly  volatile over the last two months in  fact they've been  far and away the most volatile  trading that we've seen in the offshore  bond market and they've actually  weighed on the broader bond market as  well china huarong  is one of the bigger borrowers among  chinese firms at the moment has about  40 billion dollars worth of outstanding  bonds in the onshore and offshore market  about  4 billion of that needs to be repaid or  refinanced this year  when china huarang first started selling  a lot of offshore debt  